{"records":[{"id":"hm7zy8kn94","title":"Mechanistic Insights into Autoinhibition  of the Oncogenic Chromatin Remodeler ALC1. Lehmann et al.","description":"Raw image files that form the basis for the corresponding manuscript figures. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-01-08T10:30:48.144Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hm7zy8kn94","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hm7zy8kn94"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sebastian","last_name":"Deindl","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology"],"journal_issn":["10972765"]},{"id":"zbmchm3fz4","title":"Unrestrained poly-ADP-ribosylation provides insights into chromatin regulation and human disease Prokhorova et al.","description":"ARH3/ADPRHL2 and PARG are the primary enzymes reversing ADP-ribosylation in vertebrates, yet their functions in vivo remain unclear. ARH3 is the only hydrolase able to remove serine-linked mono(ADP-ribose) (MAR) but is much less efficient than PARG against poly(ADP-ribose) (PAR) chains in vitro. Here, by using ARH3-deficient cells, we demonstrate that endogenous MARylation persists on chromatin throughout the cell cycle, including mitosis, and is surprisingly well tolerated. Conversely, persistent PARylation is highly toxic and has distinct physiological effects, in particular on active transcription histone marks such as H3K9ac and H3K27ac. Furthermore, we reveal a synthetic lethal interaction between ARH3 and PARG and identify loss of ARH3 as a mechanism of PARP inhibitor resistance, both of which can be exploited in cancer therapy. Finally, we extend our findings to neurodegeneration, suggesting that patients with inherited ARH3 deficiency suffer from stress-induced pathogenic increase in PARylation that can be mitigated by PARP inhibition.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-10T20:34:28.357Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zbmchm3fz4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zbmchm3fz4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Ahel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Molecular Biology","Cell Biology","Poly ADP Ribose Polymerase"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zxzgp65bx5","title":" PJ34 protects hair cells from cisplatin‐induced ototoxicity in vitro: possible relation to inhibition of PARP-1-AIF-mediated parthanatos","description":"PARP inhibitor PJ34 exerts anti-inflammatory and neuroprotective effects by regulating parthanatos. The objective of this study was to investigate the protective effects of PJ34 on auditory cells against cisplatin-induced injury as well as to explore the potential mechanisms involved in its action. Flow cytometry, immunofluorescence staining, Western blot, Mitochondrial membrane potential (MMP) assay and MitoSox red staining were employed to detect apoptosis, determine the expression levels of cleaved caspase-3, PARP-1, and AIF, measure MMP levels, and evaluate the levels of reactive oxygen species (ROS) in the corresponding HEI-OC1 auditory cells, ovarian cancer cell lines, and mouse cochlear hair cells. Our results demonstrated that exposure to 30 μM cisplatin activated cleaved caspase-3, PARP-1, and induced AIF nuclear translocation, leading to a decrease in MMP and an increase in ROS levels, which induced auditory cell death. Treatment with 2.5 μM PJ34 attenuated the hyperactivation of PARP-1 and nuclear translocation of AIF after cisplatin exposure, rescued the reduction of MMP and ROS accumulation, and thus protected auditory cells from cisplatin-induced cellular damage. Furthermore, PJ34 enhanced the sensitivity of ovarian cancer cell lines to cisplatin treatment. In conclusion, our findings suggest that PJ34 may attenuate cisplatin-induced hair cell death by regulating PARP-1-mediated parthanatos. Interestingly, PJ34 shows promise as a potential novel therapeutic agent for the prevention and/or treatment of cisplatin-induced ototoxicity. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-10-09T13:49:17.994Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zxzgp65bx5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zxzgp65bx5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Huiming","last_name":"Nong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiru","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yingxue","last_name":"Yuan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Junhong","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhixin","last_name":"Cao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82201293"}],"categories":["Otorhinolaryngology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6wyzgw8z8k","title":"The CST complex mediates end-protection at double-strand breaks and promotes PARP inhibitor sensitivity in BRCA1-deficient cells. DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.celrep.2018.04.046","description":"Original data used for plotting the figures belonging to the study \"The CST complex mediates end-protection at double-strand breaks and promotes PARP inhibitor sensitivity in BRCA1-deficient cells\". ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2018-05-22T12:41:39.154Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6wyzgw8z8k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6wyzgw8z8k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Sven","last_name":"Rottenberg","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Barazas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","DNA Repair","Olaparib","BRCA Gene"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yrjz46n7bc","title":"PARP1 directly disassembles nucleosomes to regulate DNA repair of Verma and Zhu et al.","description":"Upon DNA damage, chromatin remodeling is rapidly initiated to promote chromatin accessibility. Although\nthis enhanced accessibility has been linked to poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) activity, how cells\novercome the nucleosome barrier remains unclear. We discovered that PARP1 directly and asymmetrically\nevicts histone dimers proximal to DNA strand breaks from nucleosomes to generate oriented hexasomes. In\nthe presence of HPF1, PARP1 generates stable PARylated hexasomes that can facilitate recruitment of DNAand\nPAR-dependent factors. In cells, PARP activity is both required and sufficient to drive chromatin\naccessibility and the recruitment of repair factors via subnucleosomal species. Unexpectedly, we identified the\nC-terminal tail of histone H2A, a motif harboring recurrent cancer-associated mutations, as a critical\ndeterminant of efficient PARP1-mediated nucleosome disassembly. Deletion of the H2A tail sensitizes cells to\nDNA-damaging treatments, implicating a functional role of PARP1-mediated nucleosome disassembly in\nDNA repair. Together, our findings suggest that PARP1 directly drives histone eviction to form\nsubnucleosomes that facilitate efficient DNA repair.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-03T12:42:07.055Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/yrjz46n7bc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/yrjz46n7bc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hai","last_name":"Dao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04q48ey07","name":"National Institute of General Medical Sciences","grant_id":"R35GM160211"},{"id":"https://ror.org/03rx10x31","name":"American Lebanese Syrian Associated Charities","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Western Blot","Gel","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"54xr9bsxd2","title":"Metabolomic data of H460 and A549 cells after trametinib treatment","description":"MAP kinse-ERK kinase inhibitor (MEKi) resistance remains unknown. We determined gene expression profiling in MEKi-resistant cells and MEKi-sensitive cells after MEKi treatment using UHPLC-MS/MS.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-03-01T11:53:52.553Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/54xr9bsxd2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/54xr9bsxd2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"zhengke","last_name":"lian","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oncology","Metabolomics","Drug Resistance"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k5gnp6m64g","title":"Mobile origin-licensing factors confer resistance to conflicts with RNA polymerase. Scherr et al (Part 7)","description":"Mobile origin-licensing factors confer resistance to conflicts with RNA polymerase\n\nPart 7. Raw microscopy data contained in separate zip files. Each file contains tif images collected with MicroManager 1.4. \nTranscription_low-chromatin-licensed-DNA_ATP\nTranscription_low-chromatin-DNA\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-03-22T19:00:27.455Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k5gnp6m64g","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k5gnp6m64g"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Karl","last_name":"Duderstadt","orcid":"0000-0002-1279-7841"},{"first_name":"Matthias","last_name":"Scherr","orcid":"0000-0003-0110-3709"}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","Single Molecule Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"v6cszczy48","title":"Kinase Inhibitors Screen Tamoxifen Resistant Breast Cancer","description":"Antiestrogen resistance of breast cancer has been related to enhanced growth factor receptor expression and activation. We have previously shown that ectopic expression and subsequent activation of the insulin-like growth factor-1 receptor (IGF1R) or the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) in MCF7 or T47D breast cancer cells results in antiestrogen resistance. In order to identify critical therapeutic targets that mediate this antiestrogen resistance, we performed a kinase inhibitor screen with 273 different inhibitors. Various ALK inhibitors, including TEA684, AZD3463 and LDK378, inhibited cell proliferation in IGF1R expressing cells under both normal and antiestrogen resistance conditions by preventing IGF1R activation and subsequent downstream signaling; ALK inhibitors did not affect EGFR signaling. On the other hand, MEK1/2 inhibitors, including PD0325901, selumetinib, trametinib and TAK733, selectively antagonized IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance but did not affect cell proliferation under normal growth conditions. The MEK inhibitors PD0325901, selumetinib, trametinib and TAK733 caused an arrest in the G0/G1 cell cycle phase only under antiestrogen resistance conditions in IGF1R expressing cells, but not under normal growth conditions. RNAseq analysis revealed that MEK inhibitors PD0325901 and selumetinib drastically altered cell cycle progression and cell migration networks under IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance. In a group of 291 patients with primary operable ER+ breast cancer, strong pMEK staining in metastatic breast cancer after first-line tamoxifen treatment was significantly related to no clinical benefit. We propose a critical role for MEK activation in IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance and anticipate that dual-targeted therapy with MEK inhibitor and antiestrogen could improve treatment outcome.  ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-06T16:25:15.469Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v6cszczy48","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v6cszczy48"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Meerman","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Estrogen","Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor","Tamoxifen"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"wwbzvt3h3w","title":"PARP-1 and DDX21 in Breast Cancer Images","description":"These are the original microscopy images associated with Kim et al. \"Activation of PARP-1 by snoRNAs Controls Ribosome Biogenesis and Cell Growth via the RNA Helices DDX21,\" published in Molecular Cell in 2019.  These are IHC or IF images of DDX21, PARP-1, and related factors in MCF-7 cells, xenograft tumors from mice, and human breast cancer samples.  These images are from Figure 6A-B; Figure 7K, N, P; Supplemental Figure S6A, D; and Supplemental Figure S7F).\n\nSummary\nPARP inhibitors (PARPi) prevent cancer cell growth by inducing synthetic lethality with DNA repair defects (e.g., in BRCA1/2 mutant cells).  We have identified an alternate pathway for PARPi-mediated growth control in BRCA1/2-intact breast cancer cells involving rDNA transcription and ribosome biogenesis.  PARP-1 binds to snoRNAs, which stimulate PARP-1 catalytic activity in the nucleolus independent of DNA damage.  Activated PARP-1 ADP-ribosylates DDX21, an RNA helicase that localizes to nucleoli and promotes rDNA transcription when ADP-ribosylated.  Treatment with PARPi or mutation of the ADP-ribosylation sites reduce DDX21 nucleolar localization, rDNA transcription, ribosome biogenesis, protein translation, and cell growth.  The salient features of this pathway are evident in xenografts in mice and human breast cancer patient samples.  Elevated levels of PARP-1 and nucleolar DDX21 are associated with cancer-related outcomes.  Our studies provide a mechanistic rationale for efficacy of PARPi in cancer cells lacking defects in DNA repair whose growth is inhibited by PARPi.  ","source":{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-06-12T03:40:24.134Z","url":"https://elsevier.digitalcommonsdata.com/datasets/wwbzvt3h3w","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/wwbzvt3h3w"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lee","last_name":"Kraus","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f78bn3xbp7","title":"Mechanistic insights into chromatin modulation by the “orphan” remodeler ATRX. Duan et al.","description":"Uncropped gel images and blots for the manuscript (all raw images in one Powerpoint file with label) \nATRX raw data for plots (all data in one Excel file)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-07T10:20:16.249Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f78bn3xbp7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f78bn3xbp7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Suling","last_name":"Duan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tsz-Fung","last_name":"Wong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xian","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haocheng","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Huimin","last_name":"Xi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanxiang","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhong-Ping","last_name":"Yao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuanliang","last_name":"Zhai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jianyou","last_name":"Liao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Wong","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tao","last_name":"Ni","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Keda","last_name":"Zhou","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yang","last_name":"Liu","orcid":"0000-0001-7187-9196"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"32422016"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00djwmt25","name":"University Grants Committee","grant_id":"27122123"}],"categories":["Biochemistry","Structural Biology","Chromatin Remodeling"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":122830,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":129,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":122830,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":122490,"restricted":61,"embargoed":279},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2165},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":616},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":464},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":412},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":400},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":387},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":356},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":302},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":268},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":217}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":120234},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1896},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":182},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":129},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":65},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":54},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":40},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":472},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":402},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"M